            <ArticleTitle>Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>225-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4997/JRCPE.2013.307</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome (RCVS) is an underdiagnosed condition which usually presents as severe headache with or without neurological deficit. We report the case of a 55-year-old woman who presented with headache and multifocal intracerebral haemorrhage. We review the literature regarding the presentation, pathophysiology and management of RCVS and discuss how to differentiate it from cerebral vasculitis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
